## Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **`Thread.Abort` → `PlatformNotSupportedException`** | Crashes at runtime in .NET 6+. No workaround — must use cooperative cancellation. |
| **`Thread.Interrupt` only works in `WaitSleepJoin` state** | Unreliable even on Framework; obsolete in .NET 9 (`SYSLIB0046`). |
| **Original race condition** | Multiple `Enqueue` calls before the loop runs = lost items. The `WaitAny` + drain-all-items approach fixes this. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. Always wrap in `using` or call `Dispose()`. |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | The `Handle(item)` call won't be interrupted mid-execution. If `Handle` is long-running, pass the token into it and check periodically. |

---

## Recommendation

Use the **`Channel<T>`** version. It's the idiomatic .NET 8 producer-consumer pattern — thread-safe, back-pressure aware, and naturally async. It also eliminates manual locking entirely.
